# BUDGETED_DOCS.md

> Any document that feeds a model prompt declares a budget and carries its last
> measured headroom. Referenced by `AGENTS.md` and by `SHIP_CHECKLIST.md`
> section G.

---

## Why this exists

A context document is not free. Some of them are loaded automatically:

- by agent sessions, as standing instructions;
- by hosted review tools, as custom instruction files;
- by in-repository tooling that builds prompts from files on disk.

Each consumer has a hard limit. When a document crosses it, the consumer does
not degrade - it **fails**, and it fails on whatever change happened to be in
flight, which is usually not the change that grew the document.

Growth is also distributed: several units in a wave each add a paragraph to the
same counted section, none of them large, and the sum crosses the line. That is
why the budget lives in a register rather than in anyone's head.

---

## The register

| Document | Consumer | What is counted | Budget | Last measured | Headroom | Measured on |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `<PATH_TO_DOC>` | <WHICH_TOOL_OR_SESSION_LOADS_IT> | <whole file / named sections> | <LIMIT_WITH_UNITS> | <MEASURED_VALUE> | <REMAINING> | <YYYY-MM-DD> |
| `<PATH_TO_DOC>` | <CONSUMER> | <SCOPE> | <LIMIT> | <MEASURED> | <REMAINING> | <YYYY-MM-DD> |

Units are whatever the consumer actually limits - characters, tokens, bytes.
Record the unit, not a conversion; a converted number is an estimate wearing a
measurement's clothes.

---

## Measuring

```
<BUDGET_CHECK_COMMAND>
```

**If no unified check command exists yet, say so here instead of printing an
aspirational one** - a command in this file that nobody can run is a register
nobody trusts. In that case, record the per-row command or arithmetic that
produced each measured number (provenance is part of the measurement), table
which of the requirements below each existing surface meets, and file the
gaps as work items rather than asserting them done. *(The reference
deployment's register does exactly this, with three honest xs in its
requirements table.)*

Requirements for that command:

- It reports the **measured** number, not a pass/fail alone. The number is what
  goes in the table.
- It is runnable locally and in CI, with the same result.
- It fails when a document exceeds its budget, and **warns** when headroom
  falls below <WARNING_THRESHOLD>.
- It reads the budgets from one place - this file, or a configuration file this
  file points at - so the register and the gate cannot disagree.

---

## Rules

1. **Grow a counted document, measure it in the same change.** Record the new
   number in the table and in the change's report. "It is only a paragraph" is
   how the ratchet works.
2. **A warning is a finding, not a note.** Below the threshold, either trim in
   the same change or file the trim as a work item with an owner.
3. **Carving out beats trimming prose.** When a document is mostly reference
   material that agents look up rather than read, move that material to a
   separate file and link it. The reference stays; the prompt shrinks.
   *(The reference deployment carved a large index table out of its entry-point
   document for exactly this reason, and added a size test so the table could
   not drift back.)*
4. **Ratchet the size.** Once a document is trimmed, add a test that fails if
   it grows past the new ceiling. Without the ratchet, the next wave refills it.
5. **A new prompt-loading tool adds a row.** If you teach something to read a
   document into a prompt, that document is now budgeted.
6. **Never raise a ceiling to make a check pass.** Every raise is weighed
   against the consumer's real window and carries a written justification in
   the History below; the content is compressed *before* the number is
   touched. A second raise within days means the document is structurally
   full - at that point the durable alternative (per-consumer curated
   exports, or retrieval instead of a static prompt) is filed as a tracked
   work item, so the next raise has to argue against a real alternative
   instead of an absent one. *(The reference deployment's second raise in
   three days came only after the tripping rule was compressed first and the
   retrieval alternative was filed.)* Attribution corollary: when a full
   budget trips, the change that tripped it is not the cause - the file was
   full, and the next substantive addition would have hit the ceiling
   whoever wrote it.
7. **A register grows and a context window does not - plan the divergence.**
   Most hosted graders read exactly one file of yours, so everything they
   must know has to fit through that hole; size fails loudly (a rejected
   request) but shape fails silently (a skipped file looks clean). When a
   counted document nears its cap, decide deliberately - curate per-consumer
   exports, or move to retrieval - before the cap forces a silent choice
   about what the next lesson evicts.
8. **Every generator-source row is on the fast-track deny list.** A document
   a build step compiles into a prompt is code, not docs (FAST_TRACK.md);
   the two files cross-reference so neither can drift alone.

A sibling concern with the same mechanics: **numbers stated in prose** (a
count in a README, an "N of M" in a status doc) go stale unseen without a
counted-noun register deriving each from the artifact it describes - see
TESTING_STANDARDS section 8.6.

---

## History

Append a line whenever a budget or a ceiling changes, with the reason.

- `<YYYY-MM-DD>` - <document> budget set to <VALUE> because <REASON>.
- `<YYYY-MM-DD>` - <document> trimmed from <BEFORE> to <AFTER>; ratchet test
  added at <CEILING>.
